# Spec-Driven Development

Write a short specification before building anything non-trivial. The spec is the shared source of truth for what is being built, why, and how completion will be judged.

## When To Use

- Starting a new client website or major feature.
- Requirements are ambiguous or incomplete.
- The change touches multiple files, services, or user flows.
- A framework, integration, or architecture decision is needed.

Skip a full spec for typo fixes, single-line corrections, or unambiguous documentation edits.

## Process

1. State assumptions and ask for correction before proceeding.
2. Define the objective, audience, and desired outcome.
3. Document the chosen stack and commands only after they are known.
4. Define project structure, code style, testing strategy, and boundaries.
5. Convert vague requests into concrete success criteria.
6. Save durable project assumptions in `docs/project/`.

## Spec Template

```markdown
# Spec: [Project/Feature]

## Objective
[What we are building and why.]

## Users And Success Criteria
[Audience, use cases, measurable definition of done.]

## Stack And Commands
[Framework, package manager, build/test/lint/dev commands if known.]

## Structure
[Where code, tests, content, and docs should live.]

## Boundaries
- Always: [...]
- Ask first: [...]
- Never: [...]

## Open Questions
[Anything unresolved.]
```

## Verification

Before implementation, confirm the spec has concrete success criteria, clear boundaries, known open questions, and enough project context to avoid guessing.
